  "text": "Mint can feel cold without coming out of a fridge. How? Menthol, a compound found in peppermint, can activate a cold-sensitive channel called TRPM8. Cooling can activate it too. That gives us two different inputs, a temperature change and a chemical. Both can stimulate part of the same sensory machinery. So a cooling sensation does not by itself tell you the temperature of what you touched or tasted. The mint is not a tiny ice cube. Its chemistry can take another route to a cold-sensitive channel. Different input, a familiar cold sensation",
